No answer leak detected. The problem states a linear ODE system to solve.

## Solution

We solve the linear system
$$\dot{x} = x - 3y,\qquad \dot{y} = 3x + y.$$

**Matrix form.** Writing $\mathbf{z} = (x,y)^T$, the system is $\dot{\mathbf{z}} = A\mathbf{z}$ with
$$A = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & -3 \\ 3 & 1 \end{pmatrix}.$$

**Eigenvalues.** The characteristic polynomial is
$$\det(A - \lambda I) = (1-\lambda)^2 + 9 = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 10 = 0,$$
so
$$\lambda = 1 \pm 3i.$$

**Eigenvector for $\lambda = 1+3i$.** Solve $(A-(1+3i)I)\mathbf{v}=0$:
$$\begin{pmatrix} -3i & -3 \\ 3 & -3i \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}v_1\\v_2\end{pmatrix}=0.$$
The first row gives $-3i\,v_1 - 3v_2 = 0$, i.e. $v_2 = -i\,v_1$. Taking $v_1=1$ yields the eigenvector $\mathbf{v} = (1,\,-i)^T$.

**Real fundamental solutions.** The complex solution is
$$e^{(1+3i)t}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-i\end{pmatrix} = e^t(\cos 3t + i\sin 3t)\begin{pmatrix}1\\-i\end{pmatrix} = e^t\begin{pmatrix}\cos 3t + i\sin 3t\\ \sin 3t - i\cos 3t\end{pmatrix}.$$

Taking real and imaginary parts gives two real linearly independent solutions:
$$\mathbf{z}^{(1)}(t) = e^t\begin{pmatrix}\cos 3t\\ \sin 3t\end{pmatrix},\qquad \mathbf{z}^{(2)}(t) = e^t\begin{pmatrix}\sin 3t\\ -\cos 3t\end{pmatrix}.$$

**General solution.** With arbitrary constants $C_1, C_2$:
$$\boxed{\begin{aligned} x(t) &= e^t\bigl(C_1\cos 3t + C_2\sin 3t\bigr),\\ y(t) &= e^t\bigl(C_1\sin 3t - C_2\cos 3t\bigr). \end{aligned}}$$

**Verification.** Compute $\dot{x}$:
$$\dot{x} = e^t\bigl[(C_1+3C_2)\cos 3t + (C_2-3C_1)\sin 3t\bigr].$$
And $x - 3y$:
$$x-3y = e^t\bigl[(C_1+3C_2)\cos 3t + (C_2-3C_1)\sin 3t\bigr].\quad\checkmark$$

Compute $\dot{y}$:
$$\dot{y} = e^t\bigl[(3C_1-C_2)\cos 3t + (C_1+3C_2)\sin 3t\bigr].$$
And $3x+y$:
$$3x+y = e^t\bigl[(3C_1-C_2)\cos 3t + (C_1+3C_2)\sin 3t\bigr].\quad\checkmark$$

Both equations are satisfied, confirming the solution.

**Phase portrait interpretation.** The eigenvalues $1\pm 3i$ have positive real part, so the origin is an **unstable spiral (source)**. Trajectories spiral outward with angular frequency $3$ and growth rate $e^t$.

